From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Wed, 10 Sep 2026 17:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 77/77] local: spmi: pmic-arb: restore per-interrupt type and
 polarity after hibernation

After a cross-boot hibernate resume on x1e80100, all eight PMIC temp-alarm
interrupts assert continuously at ~2400/s each, every one dispatched in the
same pmic_arb_chained_irq() pass, pinning cpu0 at ~90% hardirq until the
machine is unusable. The same happens at EL1 with the plain device tree, so
it is not specific to booting the kernel at EL2.

Dumping the peripheral's own registers at probe and again on restore
(identical on all eight) names the lost state exactly:

  probe:   status=80 shdn_ctl1=08 alarm_ctl=80 rt_sts=00 set_type=01 pol_hi=01 pol_lo=01 en_set=01 latched=00
  restore: status=80 shdn_ctl1=08 alarm_ctl=80 rt_sts=00 set_type=00 pol_hi=00 pol_lo=00 en_set=01 latched=01

The peripheral is fine: status, shutdown control and alarm control read back
byte-identical, and RT_STS is clear, so nothing is actually asserting. What
changed is the interrupt configuration. SET_TYPE and both POLARITY registers
came back at power-on defaults, while irq_desc came back from the image: Linux
still has handle_edge_irq installed and believes the line is edge-triggered on
both edges, but the hardware is now level with no polarity selected.
handle_edge_irq acks LATCHED_CLR, the level input re-latches immediately, and
the cycle repeats forever -- which is exactly what latched=01 shows.

QPNPINT_REG_SET_TYPE, POLARITY_HIGH and POLARITY_LOW are only ever written
from qpnpint_irq_set_type(), which runs when the interrupt is requested and
never again. spmi-pmic-arb declares no PM ops at all, so nothing replays it.
That also explains two earlier observations: unbinding and rebinding a PMIC
driver clears the storm on its lines, because the driver re-requests its
interrupt; while re-running the peripheral's own init does not, and made it
worse, because the peripheral was never the problem.

Replay the programming for every interrupt this controller owns.
irq_set_irq_type() takes the descriptor and bus locks and calls
chip->irq_set_type() unconditionally, so re-applying the type Linux already
believes in is enough to reprogram the hardware, and no state has to be saved
across the image. .restore_noirq runs before resume_device_irqs() unmasks
anything, so no interrupt is ever delivered against the stale configuration.

This is not thermal-specific. Every interrupt behind this controller loses the
same configuration -- pmic_glink/UCSI, the power key and the RTC alarm
included -- so other post-resume PMIC misbehaviour may share this cause.

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
 drivers/spmi/spmi-pmic-arb.c | 69 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 69 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/spmi/spmi-pmic-arb.c
+++ b/drivers/spmi/spmi-pmic-arb.c
@@ -2167,12 +2167,81 @@
 };
 MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, spmi_pmic_arb_match_table);
 
+/*
+ * Hibernate restore: the PMIC comes back from the reboot with its
+ * per-interrupt configuration at power-on defaults, while irq_desc comes back
+ * from the hibernation image still describing what probe programmed. Measured
+ * on x1e80100 across a cross-boot resume, identically on all eight PMIC
+ * temp-alarm interrupts:
+ *
+ *   probe:   set_type=01 pol_hi=01 pol_lo=01 en_set=01 latched=00 rt_sts=00
+ *   restore: set_type=00 pol_hi=00 pol_lo=00 en_set=01 latched=01 rt_sts=00
+ *
+ * The peripheral itself is untouched -- its status, shutdown-control and
+ * alarm-control registers all read back identical -- and RT_STS is clear, so
+ * nothing is actually asserting. But SET_TYPE and both POLARITY registers were
+ * reset: Linux still has handle_edge_irq installed and believes the line is
+ * edge-triggered, while the hardware is level with no polarity selected.
+ * handle_edge_irq acks LATCHED_CLR, the level input re-latches immediately,
+ * and the interrupt repeats at ~2400/s per line, pinning a CPU at ~90% hardirq
+ * until the machine is unusable.
+ *
+ * Those registers are only ever written by qpnpint_irq_set_type(), i.e. once,
+ * when the interrupt is requested. That is why unbinding and rebinding a PMIC
+ * driver clears the storm on its lines -- it re-requests the interrupt -- while
+ * re-initialising the peripheral does not.
+ *
+ * Replay it for every interrupt this controller owns. irq_set_irq_type() takes
+ * the descriptor and bus locks and calls chip->irq_set_type() unconditionally,
+ * so re-applying the type Linux already believes in is enough to reprogram the
+ * hardware. This runs in .restore_noirq, before resume_device_irqs() unmasks
+ * anything, so no interrupt is ever delivered against the stale configuration.
+ *
+ * Not thermal-specific: every interrupt behind this controller is affected,
+ * pmic_glink/UCSI, the power key and the RTC alarm included.
+ */
+static int spmi_pmic_arb_restore_noirq(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct spmi_pmic_arb *pmic_arb = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	unsigned int nr = irq_get_nr_irqs();
+	unsigned int virq;
+
+	for (virq = 0; virq < nr; virq++) {
+		struct irq_data *d = irq_get_irq_data(virq);
+		unsigned int type;
+		int i;
+
+		if (!d)
+			continue;
+
+		for (i = 0; i < PMIC_ARB_MAX_BUSES; i++)
+			if (pmic_arb->buses[i] &&
+			    d->domain == pmic_arb->buses[i]->domain)
+				break;
+		if (i == PMIC_ARB_MAX_BUSES)
+			continue;
+
+		type = irqd_get_trigger_type(d);
+		if (type == IRQ_TYPE_NONE)
+			continue;
+
+		irq_set_irq_type(virq, type);
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct dev_pm_ops spmi_pmic_arb_pm_ops = {
+	.restore_noirq = pm_sleep_ptr(spmi_pmic_arb_restore_noirq),
+};
+
 static struct platform_driver spmi_pmic_arb_driver = {
 	.probe		= spmi_pmic_arb_probe,
 	.remove		= spmi_pmic_arb_remove,
 	.driver		= {
 		.name	= "spmi_pmic_arb",
 		.of_match_table = spmi_pmic_arb_match_table,
+		.pm	= pm_sleep_ptr(&spmi_pmic_arb_pm_ops),
 	},
 };
 module_platform_driver(spmi_pmic_arb_driver);
